"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[4702],{64702:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"04-dolgi","title":"Lesson 04. Good and bad debt: How to tell before signing","sourceFingerprint":"962ca292fc19e1840e00aa4940d1f8f1dcf754b65279c1748323ee8856bc3ffd","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"## Good and bad debt"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"How to tell before signing"},{"id":"b003","markdown":"Anyone who has renovated knows “we will do it this way for now and redo it later”. Temporary wiring, tiles on old adhesive, a tap without a proper filter. Sometimes it is justified: you must move in by a deadline and money is short. But until fixed, the “temporary” solution keeps reminding you of itself through short circuits or leaks. The longer you wait, the more a proper repair costs."},{"id":"b004","markdown":"A deliberate temporary solution with a clear deadline is fine. Trouble begins when so many “for now” decisions accumulate that ordinary life leaves neither energy nor money."},{"id":"b005","markdown":"Money works similarly. Debt itself is not evil; without it there would be no mortgages, factories or roads. Problems start with the wrong debts, purposes and rates. Then interest consumes every spare ruble for years. This lesson offers a test to apply before any loan and examines the cost of borrowed money in 2026."},{"id":"b006","markdown":"### What a borrowed ruble costs now"},{"id":"b007","markdown":"According to the United Credit Bureau, full credit-card borrowing costs in June 2026 were around 50% annually. Cash loans are usually cheaper than cards, but still much more expensive than deposits. The key rate on October 23 was 14%, and banks rarely lend to individuals below it outside state-subsidised programmes."},{"id":"b008","markdown":"The key loan figure is the full cost of credit, or PSK. It includes not only interest but mandatory payments: insurance when declining it raises the rate, fees and servicing. The law requires this figure in a box at the top right of the contract’s first page. Advertising shouts “from 9.9%”, while the box shows what you really pay. Compare loans only by full cost."},{"id":"b009","markdown":"WHERE THE REAL LOAN PRICE IS. ADVERT: FROM 9.9%. CONTRACT: FULL COST, INCLUDING INTEREST, INSURANCE AND FEES. COMPARE USING THAT FIGURE."},{"id":"b010","markdown":"### A test of 3 questions"},{"id":"b011","markdown":"Before borrowing, ask 3 questions. If even one answer is poor, the loan will probably consume you."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"#### What remains after the pleasure fades?"},{"id":"b013","markdown":"Some debts become something that preserves or increases value: a home, a work tool, or education that raises income. Others become a memory: a holiday, dinner, or a gadget worth half as much a year later."},{"id":"b014","markdown":"Consumer credit can leave you paying for another year after the experience has ended."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"#### Is the rate below what the purchase earns or saves?"},{"id":"b016","markdown":"An 18% loan for equipment earning 30% annually works for you. A 25% loan for something earning nothing is simply paying 25% a year to have it earlier."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"#### Could I pay if income fell by a third?"},{"id":"b018","markdown":"The most important question, and almost nobody asks it."},{"id":"b019","markdown":"Consider a simple scenario: one household salary disappears for 3 months, or your income falls by a third. Is there enough for loan payments, food and utilities? People lose jobs, fall ill and see businesses weaken. If the first setback makes the payment unaffordable, the loan is dangerous whatever its purpose."},{"id":"b020","markdown":"The Bank of Russia uses the debt-service-to-income ratio to measure this: the share of income devoted to payments on every loan."},{"id":"b021","markdown":"- Below 30%, the burden is comfortable."},{"id":"b022","markdown":"- At 30–50%, it is noticeable and mistakes already cost dearly."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"- Above 50% is a risk zone."},{"id":"b024","markdown":"- Above 80%, problems are almost guaranteed."},{"id":"b025","markdown":"The central bank limits lending to highly indebted borrowers. In QIV 2026, borrowers above 50% debt burden may account for no more than 15% of a bank’s consumer-loan issuance; those above 80% no more than 1%."},{"id":"b026","markdown":"### When debt is justified"},{"id":"b027","markdown":"Debt that increases your earning value. This is a deliberate temporary arrangement to reach a new level sooner, with a repayment plan known in advance. A course enabling a better-paid job, or a tool needed to accept an order. If the purchase pays back before the loan ends, it is an investment rather than consumption."},{"id":"b028","markdown":"An emergency. A burst pipe floods neighbours, or a pet or you need urgent surgery. Here you put out a fire rather than calculate returns. The rule still applies: the smallest amount for the shortest time. Afterwards, honestly examine why the cushion was insufficient and how to prevent a repeat."},{"id":"b029","markdown":"Housing under a subsidised programme. A family mortgage at 6% with a 14% key rate is below-market money. If you already rent and plan to remain in that city, the calculation may work. Mortgage-versus-rent arithmetic is examined in detail in the MakeCash library’s Debt chapter."},{"id":"b030","markdown":"Interest-free instalments when the money is already available. An unobvious but workable option. Suppose a work laptop costs 100 000 RUB, already held in a savings account at 13%. If the shop offers 0% over 12 months, leave the balance earning interest and pay instalments from it. Interest on the remaining money can amount to around 7 000 RUB over the year."},{"id":"b031","markdown":"Instalments have 2 catches. Since April 1, 2026, their details go to credit bureaus; in October the central bank proposed including them in debt-burden calculations, making them ordinary credit exposure. And “0%” often hides extra cost in the item’s price or mandatory insurance, so compare the outright purchase price."},{"id":"b032","markdown":"### When debt is almost certainly harmful"},{"id":"b033","markdown":"Borrowing until payday is like bailing out a boat without repairing its hole. Expenses exceed income, and borrowing does not fix that; it makes the next month shorter. This often starts a debt spiral. Taking a new loan to repay an old one, unless refinancing at a lower rate, simply increases debt and postpones the problem."},{"id":"b034","markdown":"Microloans. The legal maximum MFO rate is 0.8% daily, up to 292% annually. If you have reached this point, stop and open the next lesson. Buying something you could save for in 3–6 months: if six months of saving is impossible, its loan is probably unaffordable too. Borrowing does not make it affordable, only dearer."},{"id":"b035","markdown":"### The minimum-payment trap"},{"id":"b036","markdown":"A credit card can help if you repay the full balance before the grace period ends. You then use the bank’s money without charge. Guide 2 at the module’s end explains the careful approach. Pay only minimums, however, and the card can become your life’s most expensive loan."},{"id":"b037","markdown":"Example: card debt of 100 000 RUB, 50% annual interest, minimum payment of 5% of debt. In the first month, of the 5 000 RUB paid, about 4 170 RUB goes to interest and only 830 RUB to principal. Paying only minimums without new purchases takes almost 20 years and pays the bank over 500 000 RUB for the original 100 000 RUB spent."},{"id":"b038","markdown":"The minimum payment mainly benefits the bank: the longer you pay, the more it earns."},{"id":"b039","markdown":"Paying only the card minimum: how many years and how much in total?"},{"id":"b040","markdown":"Card debt 100 000 RUB. Annual rate 50%. Minimum payment 5% of debt. Duration 20 years."},{"id":"b041","markdown":"Until the final payment, with no further purchases."},{"id":"b042","markdown":"Total paid to the bank: 523 891 RUB. First payment: 5 000 RUB, including 4 167 RUB interest and 833 RUB principal. Spending 100 000 RUB leads to repayment of 523 891 RUB, or 5.2 times as much. The minimum mainly benefits the bank. Banks impose a cash floor, here 1 000 RUB; without it, repaying 5% of the balance would continue indefinitely."},{"id":"b043","markdown":"### Time is a price too"},{"id":"b044","markdown":"A longer term reduces monthly payment but increases overpayment. Consider a 300 000 RUB loan at 25% annually:"},{"id":"b045","markdown":"| Term | Monthly payment | Overpayment |\\n| 1 year | about 28 500 RUB | about 42 000 RUB |\\n| 2 years | about 16 000 RUB | about 84 000 RUB |\\n| 3 years | about 11 900 RUB | about 129 000 RUB |\\n| 5 years | about 8 800 RUB | about 228 000 RUB |\\nThe bank happily offers 5 years: 8 800 RUB monthly seems manageable. Almost 1.8 times the amount in total remains in small print. Before any loan, multiply payment by number of months: that is the real purchase price."},{"id":"b046","markdown":"A loan at 25% annually: what changes with the term?"},{"id":"b047","markdown":"Loan amount: 300 000 RUB. Term:"},{"id":"b048","markdown":"1 year, 2 years, 3 years, 5 years. Monthly payment: 8 805 RUB."},{"id":"b049","markdown":"That is what the bank offers."},{"id":"b050","markdown":"Interest overpayment: 228 324 RUB. Real purchase price, payment \xd7 months: 528 324 RUB. The monthly payment seems manageable, yet total repayment is 1.8 times the amount borrowed."},{"id":"b051","markdown":"### Legal protections available"},{"id":"b052","markdown":"#### Cooling-off period"},{"id":"b053","markdown":"Since September 1, 2025, most consumer-loan funds are delayed: loans from 50 000 to 200 000 RUB by 4 hours, and above 200 000 RUB by 48 hours. During this period you may decline without consequences. It also applies to credit-card limit increases."},{"id":"b054","markdown":"The period does not cover mortgages, car loans transferred to the seller, or refinancing without a debt increase. Essentially it embeds lesson 2’s 72-hour rule in law. Use those hours."},{"id":"b055","markdown":"#### Voluntary credit restriction"},{"id":"b056","markdown":"Since March 1, 2025, Gosuslugi lets you prohibit loans to yourself fully or partly, such as online loans or MFO borrowing only. According to the United Credit Bureau, by August 2026, 21.66 million Russians had set a restriction, and more than half already had loans outstanding."},{"id":"b057","markdown":"It protects against fraudsters borrowing in your name and your own bad-day decisions. Gosuslugi also allows removal, but not instantly, so a fraudster cannot persuade you to do it in 5 minutes."},{"id":"b058","markdown":"#### Cancelling insurance"},{"id":"b059","markdown":"Insurance sold with a loan may be cancelled within 30 days. Check the contract first: the bank may raise the interest rate without insurance, so calculate which option is cheaper."},{"id":"b060","markdown":"### The essentials"},{"id":"b061","markdown":"- Compare loans by full cost of credit; an advertised rate tells you little."},{"id":"b062","markdown":"- Before borrowing, ask 3 questions: what remains after buying, is the rate below the purchase’s return, and can you pay if income falls by a third?"},{"id":"b063","markdown":"- Credit-card minimum payments can stretch debt over 20 years and multiply repayment by 5."},{"id":"b064","markdown":"- Multiply payment by term. Use the cooling-off period and consider a voluntary restriction."},{"id":"b065","markdown":"Next lesson examines what to do when harmful debts already exist."}],"terms":[{"id":"klyuchevaya-stavka","term":"Key interest rate","definition":"The rate at which the central bank lends to banks. The main monetary-policy tool and the economy’s “price of money”. A higher rate makes loans more expensive and slows inflation."},{"id":"kreditnaya-istoriya","term":"Credit history","definition":"A record of how you repaid loans. A poor or absent history means rejection or a rate above the market. An empty history is worse than a history of repaid loans."},{"id":"mfo","term":"Microfinance organisations (MFOs)","definition":"Companies lending to people who do not interest banks. After the banking-sector clean-up, their number in Russia exceeded 1000."},{"id":"psk","term":"Full cost of credit (PSK)","definition":"The equivalent of an effective interest rate: the real cost of a loan including every payment. Banks must disclose it, though in small print. The only honest comparison figure."},{"id":"refinansirovanie","term":"Refinancing","definition":"A new loan at a lower rate to repay an old one. It can help when the rate difference exceeds 1.5–2% and substantial time remains. It makes sense in the first half of the term."},{"id":"semeynaya-ipoteka","term":"Family mortgage","definition":"A state-subsidised mortgage programme for families with children: the bank lends below market rates and the budget compensates the difference. The rate and limit depend on children and region. Terms change often, becoming stricter each time."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"04-dolgi-q1","q":"A loan advert says “from 9.9%”. Which figure reveals the actual cost?","options":["The advertised rate","The monthly payment","Full cost of credit in the box on the contract’s first page"],"correct":2},{"id":"04-dolgi-q2","q":"Card debt is 100 000 RUB at 50% annually and you pay only a 5 000 RUB minimum. What happens in the first month?","options":["Most goes to interest; principal falls by about 830 RUB","Principal falls by 5 000 RUB","No interest accrues if the minimum is paid"],"correct":0},{"id":"04-dolgi-q3","q":"You arranged a 300 000 RUB loan and changed your mind after 2 hours. What can you do?","options":["Nothing: the contract is signed","Decline without consequences during the cooling-off period","Only repay early with extra cost"],"correct":1}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);